{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t01-019",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "CLR execution IL JIT AOT assemblies reflection attributes and loading",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "C# source is compiled by the SDK toolchain into assemblies containing metadata and intermediate language (IL). The Common Language Runtime (CLR) loads managed code, resolves dependencies, verifies or validates execution constraints, manages memory, and coordinates runtime services.",
    "The JIT compiler turns IL into native code as methods are needed. Tiered compilation and profile-guided optimization can replace early code with more optimized code. ReadyToRun and Native AOT move some or all compilation earlier, with different startup, size, portability, and dynamic-code tradeoffs.",
    "An assembly is a deployment and identity unit containing metadata, IL or native code, and resources. Assembly identity, dependency resolution, load context, and version policy are related but distinct; matching an assembly name alone does not prove type identity across load contexts.",
    "Reflection inspects runtime metadata and can invoke members dynamically, while attributes attach declarative metadata for tools and frameworks. Reflection is powerful but can incur runtime cost, trimming risks, and Native AOT incompatibilities; source generation or explicit registration may be better for known types.",
    "Dynamic loading belongs behind a deliberate plugin boundary. `AssemblyLoadContext` isolates dependency resolution and can support unloading only when all references to the collectible context and its types are released. It is not a security sandbox."
  ],
